{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-116178",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/632d27d8354cdde40f59894fc017c49a49d47003d2f914ed0deef8c6751d7c1d.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to -2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{y+2} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =-\\frac{3}{y+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only two that give this behavior are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =-\\frac{3}{y+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, the slopes of the slope field are positive for all $y\\ne -2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{3}{y+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y>-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne -2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
